#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# eigenvectors

#% text
# `eigenvectors(m)` gives one eigenvector for each eigenvalue of the square matrix `m`, in the order of `eigenvalues(m)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A repeated eigenvalue gets as many independent eigenvectors as it has; a defective eigenvalue (one with fewer) gets zero vectors for the missing ones.

#% item
# Exact matrices give exact vectors (with algebraic numbers where needed); machine matrices give numerical vectors of length 1, each with its entry of largest modulus positive (real).

#% item
# A real symmetric matrix of numbers with a precision (`n(m, 30)`) is solved at that precision, and its eigenvalues and eigenvectors carry it; other such matrices are solved in machine arithmetic.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
eigenvectors([[2, 0], [0, 3]])
#% output
#= [[0, 1], [1, 0]]

#% cell
eigenvectors([[1, 2], [2, 1]])
#% output
#= [[1, 1], [-1, 1]]

#% cell
eigenvectors([[2.0, 1.0], [1.0, 3.0]])
#% output
#= [[0.5257311121191336, 0.8506508083520401], [0.8506508083520401, -0.5257311121191336]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A defective matrix, with one eigenvector for a double eigenvalue:

#% cell
eigenvectors([[1, 1], [0, 1]])
#% output
#= [[1, 0], [0, 0]]

#% text
# A rotation has complex eigenvectors:

#% cell
eigenvectors([[0, -1], [1, 0]])
#% output
#= [[im, 1], [-im, 1]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Each vector satisfies `m.v == lambda*v`:

#% cell
m = [[4, 1], [2, 3]]; vs = eigenvectors(m); ls = eigenvalues(m); [dot(m, vs[1]) - ls[1]*vs[1], dot(m, vs[2]) - ls[2]*vs[2]]
#% output
#= [[0, 0], [0, 0]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `eigenvalues`, `eigensystem`, `characteristic_polynomial`
